      "id": 1,
      "text": "Golovin takes its name from Russian Vice‑Admiral Vasily Golovnin, who explored Alaska in the early 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golovin%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Golovin was originally an Inupiat village called Chiŋik, reflecting the area's long indigenous heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golovin%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic Swedish Covenant Mission, a reindeer station, marks Golovin’s early 20th‑century settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/golovin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Reindeer herding began in Golovin, thanks to the mission’s pioneering efforts that introduced the practice to the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/golovin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Golovin sits on a point between Golovnin Bay and Golovnin Lagoon on the Seward Peninsula.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golovin%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Golovin lies about 105 km east of Nome, a classic Alaskan drive.",
